kosha carbonyl

  -- C=O group: carbon double-bonded to oxygen. the most reactive organic group.
  -- the C=O bond is polarised: C is electrophilic (δ+), O is nucleophilic (δ-).
  -- this polarity drives nucleophilic addition — the fundamental reaction of carbonyls.
  --
  -- two forms depending on carbon's other bonds:
  --   aldehyde: -CHO (carbonyl at end of chain). more reactive.
  --             formaldehyde HCHO, acetaldehyde CH3CHO.
  --   ketone:   >C=O (carbonyl in middle of chain). less reactive.
  --             acetone (CH3)2CO. propanone.
  --
  -- reactions:
  --   nucleophilic addition: Nu attacks C → addition product
  --   oxidation: aldehyde → carboxylic acid (Tollens test, silver mirror)
  --   reduction: C=O → C-OH (NaBH4, LiAlH4)
  --   aldol condensation: two carbonyls → β-hydroxy carbonyl
  --   Grignard: R-MgX + C=O → tertiary alcohol
  --
  -- metabolic importance: glucose is a polyhydroxy aldehyde.
  -- pyruvate, acetyl-CoA — all carry carbonyl groups.
  -- the Krebs cycle is fundamentally carbonyl chemistry.

